post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
smcbatt is built by DKMS for every installed kernel 7.3 and newer. Older
kernels still ship applesmc and ACPI SBS, so DKMS skips them on purpose.

The unit is enabled but not started here: the DKMS hook may not have built the
module yet. Start it once, or reboot:
  sudo systemctl start smcbatt.service

The charge limit ships as 80% in /etc/battlimit.conf and is applied at boot by
battlimit.service (also enabled here). Change it with:

  sudo battlimit set 80     # apply and remember
  sudo battlimit off        # back to 100%
  battlimit                 # show current and configured

Check it:
  lsmod | grep smcbatt
  upower -i /org/freedesktop/UPower/devices/battery_macsmc_battery
  cat /sys/class/power_supply/macsmc-battery/charge_control_end_threshold
EOF

  if [ -d /run/systemd/system ]; then
    systemctl enable smcbatt.service battlimit.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    systemctl start battlimit.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  fi
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}

pre_remove() {
  if [ -d /run/systemd/system ]; then
    systemctl disable --now smcbatt.service battlimit.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  fi
  # Leaving the SMC limited after the package is gone would be a nasty surprise.
  if [ -e /sys/class/power_supply/macsmc-battery/charge_control_end_threshold ]; then
    printf '100\n' > /sys/class/power_supply/macsmc-battery/charge_control_end_threshold 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
}
